WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.091
How a working team uses code graders.

2
00:00:02.371 --> 00:00:11.203
In a real team, code graders live in the repository next to the feature, run in CI on every change, and are reviewed like any other code.

3
00:00:11.483 --> 00:00:14.981
A few practices separate useful suites from noisy ones.

4
00:00:15.261 --> 00:00:17.757
Start from failure modes, not from metrics.

5
00:00:18.037 --> 00:00:29.088
Each grader should map to a failure you found in error analysis (“replies promise exceptions the policy doesn’t allow”), so a failing check points at a known problem with a known owner.

6
00:00:29.368 --> 00:00:37.560
Hamel Husain describes this layer as the unit tests of an LLM product: scoped, fast assertions that run constantly.

7
00:00:37.840 --> 00:00:39.739
Decompose before you delegate.

8
00:00:40.019 --> 00:00:41.363
“Is this reply good?

9
00:00:41.643 --> 00:00:43.861
” sounds like a job for a model judge.

10
00:00:44.141 --> 00:00:45.187
Much of it isn’t.

11
00:00:45.467 --> 00:00:47.643
Does the reply mention the order ID?

12
00:00:47.923 --> 00:00:49.885
Is it under 120 words?

13
00:00:50.165 --> 00:00:52.235
Does it avoid the forbidden promise list?

14
00:00:52.515 --> 00:00:54.755
Does every number in it appear in the policy?

15
00:00:55.035 --> 00:00:56.400
Each of those is code.

16
00:00:56.680 --> 00:00:59.389
What remains (does it actually answer the question?)

17
00:00:59.669 --> 00:01:05.536
is a much narrower judgment, which makes the judge in the next lesson cheaper and easier to calibrate.

18
00:01:05.816 --> 00:01:07.096
Watch for brittleness.

19
00:01:07.376 --> 00:01:18.043
When a code grader starts failing outputs that a person would accept, that’s a grader bug, and you fix it with a new test case, not by loosening the check until the dashboard turns green.

20
00:01:18.323 --> 00:01:26.131
On model selection: deterministic graders are model-agnostic, which is what makes them valuable when you compare providers.

21
00:01:26.411 --> 00:01:37.184
Swap in a cheaper model or another provider’s model, rerun the same checks, and the difference you see comes from the model, not from a judge that might prefer its own family’s style.
